// ── How I Think ──
const philosophy = [
	{
		headline: "Find the constraint first.",
		body: "Every system has a bottleneck. The Theory of Constraints teaches that unless you're directly addressing the thing that's actually limiting throughput, every other improvement is an illusion. I apply this to everything: client IT strategy, internal operations, sales conversations, and my own prioritization. The first question is always the same. What's the one thing holding this back?",
	},
	{
		headline: "Make the work visible.",
		body: "Unplanned work is the silent killer of IT operations. I'm influenced heavily by The Phoenix Project and improvement kata, the idea that every task should be visible, tracked, and intentional. When work is invisible, priorities are imaginary. I build systems and processes that force clarity: what are we doing, why, and what's blocked.",
	},
	{
		headline: "Build when it gives you control. Buy when it saves your sanity.",
		body: "I'm not dogmatic about building everything in-house. If a vendor solves the problem well and shifts the maintenance burden off my team, that's often the right call. But when control and long-term ROI matter, when the integration layer is the product, I build it myself. The automation platform I've built on N8n exists because no off-the-shelf tool could tie our stack together the way we needed. Knowing when to build and when to buy is one of the most important judgment calls in operations.",
	},
	{
		headline: "Unknown is never okay.",
		body: "In production automation and AI tooling, silent assumptions break things fast. When a cross-vendor diagnostic checks three network platforms and one does not answer, the tool reports the coverage gap honestly instead of returning green. Every write tool I ship separates plan from execute, reads back what actually landed, and logs the mutation to an audit ledger. Safety comes from mechanisms enforced in code, never from hoping a prompt behaves.",
	},
	{
		headline: "Orchestrate the work, don't grind through it solo.",
		body: "The same discipline that scales an operations team scales AI-assisted engineering. Lock down the architecture and invariants before writing code, isolate parallel streams in git worktrees, gate merges behind real automated tests and CI checks, and review every diff. Software and operations fail for the exact same reason: when durable state lives in someone's head instead of tracked files on disk.",
	},
];

// ── Highlighted Work ──
const highlights = [
	{
		label: "The Automation Layer",
		title: "Built an automation platform that replaced our entire integration stack.",
		body: "I designed and built a workflow engine on N8n that processes PSA ticket webhooks, routes security alerts through conditional logic based on JSON payload structure, enriches ticket data with AI, and syncs billing information across systems. It replaced multiple Zapier workflows, reduced manual intervention, and gave us full control over how our tools communicate. It's not pretty code. It's effective code, and it runs every day without complaint.",
	},
	{
		label: "Constraint-Based Sales",
		title: "Redesigned our entire sales approach around one question: what's your constraint?",
		body: 'I rebuilt our sales deck and discovery process around three core business metrics: financial accuracy, operational efficiency, and legal compliance, all anchored by the Theory of Constraints. Instead of leading with a feature list and hoping something sticks, we ask prospects about their business goals and constraints, then draw a direct line from IT to outcomes. It repositioned us from "IT support company" to "strategic business partner" in every conversation.',
	},
	{
		label: "Full-Stack Operations Buildout",
		title: "Built the operational backbone of a growing MSP from scratch.",
		body: "SOPs for helpdesk, dispatch, escalation, and project management. Legal frameworks including MSA and services guide. Vendor stack evaluation and deployment, from RMM to PSA to compliance tooling. Technical documentation library. Engineer training programs. Sales process. I didn't inherit a playbook. I wrote one, and then trained the team to run it. Most of it was built solo, under pressure, with shifting priorities. And it works.",
	},
	{
		label: "AI Operations",
		title: "Gave AI real hands on the MSP stack, then put it to work.",
		body: "I built MCP servers across our PSA, RMM, documentation, M365, and voice systems, then unified them behind a white-labeled gateway with scoped access keys. On top of that: a production AI teammate that lives in Teams, and a multi-agent orchestration harness that runs my daily operations. The AI doesn't advise from the sidelines. It reads tickets, files documentation, and executes runbooks. Governed, logged, and reviewed.",
	},
	{
		label: "MCP & Entity Graph Architecture",
		title: "Unified 8+ vendor platforms into one PostgreSQL entity graph and 30+ MCP servers.",
		body: "I architected a production monorepo of 34 isolated FastMCP servers covering PSA, RMM, documentation, M365, security, billing, networking, and VoIP, all routed behind our maintained open-source fork of the MetaMCP gateway. Underneath sits a PostgreSQL 16 entity graph spanning 130+ Alembic migrations that ingests and merges users, endpoints, cameras, network gear, and inventory across Autotask, IT Glue, NinjaRMM, CIPP/Intune, UniFi, Meraki, WatchGuard, and Domotz via MAC and identity resolution. Engineers and AI agents query one unified record instead of tabbing across ten vendor portals.",
	},
	{
		label: "Autonomous Agents & v2 Alerting",
		title: "Shipped a Teams-native AI teammate and a Postgres-backed alert pipeline.",
		body: "I deployed an isolated production Hermes AI agent integrated directly into Microsoft Teams with tenant-gated message validation, an immutable safety floor baked into its container image, and a two-way git-synced brain repository for skills and institutional memory. Alongside it runs a v2 alerting and ticket-writing pipeline backed by a dedicated operational Postgres schema for alert deduplication, ticket memory, AI-assisted classification, and human-in-the-loop rule grading.",
	},
	{
		label: "Edge AI Platform",
		title: "Built Keystone, a client-facing AI discovery portal on Cloudflare Workers.",
		body: "Ported my open-source Hatchling conversation engine to the edge as a single Cloudflare Worker serving a public client portal and an Access-gated admin SPA. Staff generate unique links or phone-readable entry codes; clients complete guided Theory-of-Constraints discovery interviews while the model writes structured intelligence files through tool calls. Per-session Durable Objects serialize turns and enforce budgets, D1 stores every artifact, and Turnstile plus rate limiting guard the unauthenticated entry points.",
	},
	{
		label: "Cloud & GitOps Infrastructure",
		title: "Provisioned a zero-SSH production cloud with Terraform, SOPS/age, and Grafana.",
		body: "I defined our multi-host Linode cloud environment in Terraform and cloud-init, fronted entirely by Cloudflare Tunnels and Access with SOPS/age-encrypted secrets stored in a separate vault repository. Pull-based systemd GitOps timers and Watchtower keep containers current straight from GHCR with no CI SSH keys anywhere. Paired it with a Grafana and Loki observability stack, provisioned LogQL alerts, and a 260+ script PowerShell RMM library for endpoint automation.",
	},
	{
		label: "M365 Policy-as-Code & Open Source",
		title: "Turned a 70+ hour Microsoft 365 tenant buildout into repeatable CIPP baselines.",
		body: "Redesigned multi-tenant Microsoft 365 onboarding from a manual senior-engineer project into versioned, two-tier Conditional Access, Intune, Purview, and CIPP baseline templates that push cleanly to fresh tenants. When the open-source tools we depend on hit gaps, I ship fixes back upstream: maintaining our public MetaMCP fork with 37+ stars and contributing anti-phishing baseline improvements to CyberDrain's CIPP.",
	},
	{
		label: "Pro-Bono Nonprofit AI Rollout",
		title: "Led a regional nonprofit's Claude Team rollout and built Entra-backed OAuth 2.1 connectors.",
		body: "Volunteered end-to-end technical leadership to roll out Claude Team across a local nonprofit, authoring their org instructions, shared skills, and staff governance playbook. Because their volunteer management platform had no native Claude integration, I engineered a split OAuth 2.1 authorization hub and Cloudflare Workers MCP connector suite federated to their Microsoft Entra ID tenant over private Service Bindings, complete with hourly refresh revocation, Durable Object write ledgers, preview-and-readback safety, D1 audit trails, and an admin kill-switch dashboard.",
	},
	{
		label: "Internal Web Applications",
		title: "Replaced legacy reports and spreadsheet quoting with custom Astro and React apps.",
		body: "Built purpose-built web applications where off-the-shelf MSP tooling fell short: a VoIP Configure-Price-Quote SPA in Astro, React 19, and TypeScript with real-time seat tier bundling, margin math backed by 42 Vitest unit tests, and direct n8n webhook submission; a Theory-of-Constraints operational reporting dashboard replacing legacy PSA reports; and a custom WordPress connector plugin giving programmatic control over our marketing site.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="About — Alex Ivantsov | Operations Manager & Systems Engineer"
	description="Operations Manager and Systems Engineer specializing in MSP operations, automation, cybersecurity, and IT strategy. Building systems that make technology serve the people who depend on it."
>
	<Nav currentPath="/about" />

	<main class="px-6 pt-24 md:pt-36 pb-12">
		<!-- ═══ 1. Hero ═══ -->
		<section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-16 text-center">
			<div class="about-fade" style="--delay: 0">
				<div
					class="w-44 h-44 mx-auto mb-6 rounded-full overflow-hidden border-2 border-white/10 shadow-lg shadow-cyan-900/10"
				>
					<img
						src={profileImage.src}
						alt="Alex Ivantsov"
						class="w-full h-full object-cover"
					transition:name="profile"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="about-fade" style="--delay: 80">
				<h1
					class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3"
				>
					<span class="font-sans text-white">Alex </span><span
						class="font-serif italic text-shimmer"
						transition:name="name-accent"
						>Ivantsov</span
					>
				</h1>
			</div>

			<div class="about-fade" style="--delay: 160">
				<p
					class="text-lg md:text-xl font-bold text-white/[0.64]"
				>
					Operations Manager. Systems Engineer. Builder of Things
					That Work.
				</p>
			</div>

			<!-- Scale / Proof strip -->
			<div
				class="about-fade grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-3 mt-10 text-left"
				style="--delay: 240"
			>
				{
					scaleMetrics.map((metric) => (
						<div class="rounded-2xl bg-[#0f1420] border border-white/[0.05] px-4 py-4">
							<p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-extrabold font-mono text-cyan-400 tracking-tight mb-1">
								{metric.value}
							</p>
							<p class="text-xs font-semibold text-white/[0.78]">
								{metric.label}
							</p>
							<p class="text-[0.65rem] font-mono text-white/[0.38] mt-0.5">
								{metric.detail}
							</p>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>

			<!-- Quick section jump links -->
			<div
				class="about-fade flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 mt-7 text-[0.65rem] font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em]"
				style="--delay: 300"
			>
				<a
					href="#about"
					class="text-white/[0.38] hover:text-cyan-400/80 transition-colors"
					>About</a
				>
				<a
					href="#capabilities"
					class="text-white/[0.38] hover:text-cyan-400/80 transition-colors"
					>What I Do</a
				>
				<a
					href="#expertise"
					class="text-white/[0.38] hover:text-cyan-400/80 transition-colors"
					>Technical Expertise</a
				>
				<a
					href="#principles"
					class="text-white/[0.38] hover:text-cyan-400/80 transition-colors"
					>How I Think</a
				>
				<a
					href="#work"
					class="text-white/[0.38] hover:text-cyan-400/80 transition-colors"
					>Shipped Work</a
				>
				<a
					href="/projects"
					class="text-cyan-400/70 hover:text-cyan-300 transition-colors"
					>Projects &rarr;</a
				>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="section-divider divider-reveal max-w-5xl mx-auto mb-16"
		></div>

		<!-- ═══ 2. About Me ═══ -->
		<section id="about" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
			<div class="scroll-reveal">
				<h2
					class="text-[0.65rem] font-mono text-cyan-400/[0.55] uppercase tracking-[0.3em] mb-6"
				>
					About Me
				</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="space-y-5">
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					I've spent my career in the managed services space,
					where IT meets real business operations. Started out
					as an engineer solving technical problems, and
					somewhere along the way I became the person solving
					business problems with technology.
				</p>
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					Today I lead operations at a growing MSP. My role
					crosses engineering, sales, compliance, and strategy,
					often in the same afternoon. I build the automation
					workflows that connect our tools, run the client
					relationships, design the compliance programs, and
					train the team that delivers it all. Lately I've
					been building the AI layer that works alongside
					them: agents with real access to our tools, doing
					real work under real governance.
				</p>
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					I'm not a specialist who goes deep on one thing. I'm
					a generalist who goes deep on whatever matters most at
					the time. I've gotten comfortable operating where
					technical execution meets business strategy, and the
					work I care about most is finding the constraint: the
					one thing actually holding an operation back, and
					solving that before anything else.
				</p>
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					I think the MSP model is underrated. We sit at the
					workflow layer, not the feature layer. We see the whole
					picture: finance, operations, compliance, IT. We can
					draw direct lines between technology decisions and
					business outcomes. That perspective shapes everything
					I do.
				</p>
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					Over the past few years that builder streak pulled me
					deep into full-stack platform engineering and AI
					infrastructure. On any given week I might ship Python
					FastMCP servers, write Alembic migrations for a
					cross-vendor PostgreSQL entity graph, maintain an
					open-source MCP gateway fork, provision Linode and
					Cloudflare infrastructure in Terraform, or deploy
					TypeScript Workers with OAuth 2.1 federation and
					Durable Objects. I like owning the whole line from
					strategy and schema design down to the container and
					the alert rule.
				</p>
				<p class="scroll-reveal text-base md:text-lg text-white/[0.56] leading-relaxed">
					That work doesn't stop at the office door. Outside the
					day job I build financial signals intelligence software,
					run a multi-agent Proxmox homelab, publish open-source
					tooling for other operators, and volunteer hands-on AI
					adoption and custom connector engineering for a local
					nonprofit.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="section-divider divider-reveal max-w-5xl mx-auto mb-16"
		></div>

		<!-- ═══ 3. What I Do ═══ -->
		<section id="capabilities" class="max-w-5xl mx-auto mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
			<div class="scroll-reveal mb-8">
				<h2
					class="text-[0.65rem] font-mono text-cyan-400/[0.55] uppercase tracking-[0.3em]"
				>
					What I Do
				</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
				{
					roles.map((role, i) => (
						<div
							class={`scroll-reveal ${i % 2 === 0 ? "from-left" : "from-right"} rounded-2xl bg-[#0f1420] border border-white/[0.05] p-6`}
